Secure decentralized messaging**


## Ancient & Forgotten Internet Protocols (Retro Stuff)

### Gopher
-   **Purpose:** Gopher is a distributed document search protocol that predates the World Wide Web, organizing information into a hierarchical menu system.
-   **How it Works:** Gopher clients connect to Gopher servers and navigate through menus of links. Each menu item points to another Gopher server or resource. The protocol uses a simple text-based format and runs on TCP port 70. Gopher was largely superseded by HTTP.
-   **Key Features:**
    -   **Hierarchical Menus:** Structured navigation.
    -   **Simple Protocol:** Easy to implement.
    -   **Document-Oriented:** Focused on text resources.
-   **Use Cases:** Historical research, legacy systems, alternative to web.
-   **Related Protocols:**
